TSKlNIgK1P4	https://unsplash.com/photos/TSKlNIgK1P4	https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1579818276639-342e7f374413	2020-01-23 22:31:12.731524	t	5000	5000	1	Truly a river of ice, Antarctica's relatively fast-moving Byrd Glacier courses through the Transantarctic Mountains at a rate of 0.8 kilometers (0.5 miles) per year. More than 180 kilometers (112 miles) long, the glacier flows down from the polar plateau (left) to the Ross Ice Shelf (right). Long, sweeping flow lines are crossed in places by much shorter lines, which are deep cracks in the ice called crevasses. The conspicuous red patches indicate areas of exposed rock.	usgs	USGS								Byrd Glacier, Antarctica	-80.333333	158.9999999	Antarctica		1805339	8480	white and red abstract painting					LDLYZfkS?]XA01%g_2M{^0WXM|R%
